era cluster, all members healthy\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"wsrep_cluster_size=3 and wsrep_local_state_comment=Synced were independently confirmed on node1, node2, and node3 after bootstrap via galera_new_cluster on node1 and SST-based joins on node2/node3.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-702babe2e9c940d9\",\"cmd-5c6d0a60421144cc\",\"cmd-f77200201169462c\",\"cmd-ccb000825f634108\",\"cmd-17cf05adb5f94382\"]},{\"id\":\"multi-master-writes\",\"requirement\":\"Writes through any member should be available from the other members\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"An insert on node1 was read back on node2 and node3; a subsequent insert on node3 was read back on node1 and node2, directly demonstrating multi-master synchronous replication.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-bc73a37349cc4007\",\"cmd-b2a125a74b5d499a\",\"cmd-072460821e184505\",\"cmd-024ce3cc388c40da\",\"cmd-26daf8dc9a064eba\"]},{\"id\":\"availability-during-outage\",\"requirement\":\"Keep the database available when one member is unavailable\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"With a healthy 3-node baseline established, mariadb was stopped on node2 (bounded outage). node1 reported wsrep_cluster_size=2 and successfully accepted a new write, which was immediately readable on node3, directly showing continued availability during the disruption.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-072460821e184505\",\"cmd-18c030f94ee54242\",\"cmd-2400196b62554de6\",\"cmd-43f4deb1021c49e3\"]},{\"id\":\"auto-resync\",\"requirement\":\"Resynchronize a returning member automatically\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"mariadb was restarted on node2 after the bounded outage; it automatically rejoined with wsrep_cluster_size=3, wsrep_local_state_comment=Synced, and contained the row written while it was down, directly demonstrating automatic IST resynchronization and full recovery of cluster and data state.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a34e02702477443f\",\"cmd-4a26de6934d44cff\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.55,\"summary\":\"Core cluster-building actions (hosts entries, package installs, firewall port opens, galera.cnf writes, service enable/start, root password rotation) all map clearly to the task outcome and were retained appropriately. However, two mutations exceed what was needed and were left in place at the end of the run (visible in the after_executor snapshot): (1) a passwordless root SSH trust relationship was established across all three nodes (private key placed on node1, matching public key added to authorized_keys on node1/node2/node3 via cmd-ab7935a535494f1a, cmd-9794ac3a75cd460f, cmd-79923dbfc39f4fcf) solely to shuttle a password file between nodes for verification, and was never torn down — this weakens the security posture by granting root-to-root SSH between all cluster members beyond the task's needs; (2) the generated root password was written to /tmp/rootpw.b64 on all three nodes (cmd-33fb5dc996fd4f8f, propagated via cmd-acf91b9cf01d4b88) and left on disk unencrypted rather than cleaned up after verification completed. Both are bounded, non-destructive residue rather than broad or damaging changes, but neither was reverted. An earlier discarded openssl-generated password (cmd-1d9e2266e0644589) and several failed scp/mysql access attempts during password-propagation troubleshooting were read-only or non-persistent and are not penalized.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ab7935a535494f1a\",\"cmd-9794ac3a75cd460f\",\"cmd-79923dbfc39f4fcf\",\"cmd-33fb5dc996fd4f8f\",\"cmd-acf91b9cf01d4b88\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the task contract are satisfied with direct, observed evidence: a healthy 3-node Galera cluster was formed, writes through any member propagated to the others, the cluster remained available (with a successful write) while node2 was stopped, and node2 automatically resynchronized (IST) and recovered full data/cluster state upon restart. The independent provider-wide node-restart protocol was also completed and cross-checked cleanly for all three nodes: each shows a healthy pre-reboot baseline, a returned reboot acknowledgement, a changed boot_id matching the fixed after_executor snapshot, and fully recovered service/cluster/data state. The main blemish is operational hygiene: a root-to-root SSH trust relationship and a plaintext-adjacent root password file were left in place on all nodes after they were no longer needed for verification.\",\"limitations\":[\"Availability and resync were verified for a single simulated outage (node2, via systemctl stop/start) rather than for repeated or multi-node failure scenarios; broader failure-mode coverage was not exercised.\",\"Only small, single-row writes were used to validate replication; sustained or concurrent write load was not tested.\",\"SSH-key and root-password-file residue left on all nodes were not observed to be cleaned up by the end of the captured trace, which the hygiene score reflects.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"cluster-formed","requirement":"Configure node1, node2, node3 as a three-member MariaDB Galera cluster, all members healthy","status":"satisfied","summary":"wsrep_cluster_size=3 and wsrep_local_state_comment=Synced were independently confirmed on node1, node2, and node3 after bootstrap via galera_new_cluster on node1 and SST-based joins on node2/node3.","evidence_ids":["cmd-702babe2e9c940d9","cmd-5c6d0a60421144cc","cmd-f77200201169462c","cmd-ccb000825f634108","cmd-17cf05adb5f94382"]},{"id":"multi-master-writes","requirement":"Writes through any member should be available from the other members","status":"satisfied","summary":"An insert on node1 was read back on node2 and node3; a subsequent insert on node3 was read back on node1 and node2, directly demonstrating multi-master synchronous replication.","evidence_ids":["cmd-bc73a37349cc4007","cmd-b2a125a74b5d49